Added the OAuth clients control panel at `/controlpanel/identity-clients`. It registers and unregisters clients, enables and disables them, rotates their secrets, and rotates the signing key — the frontend half of the `@identity-clients` and `@identity-keys` endpoints.

The interesting part is the secret. It exists in exactly one response, the one that mints it at registration or rotation, and the server cannot read it back. So the panel does not treat it like a field: it is held in the container rather than read from the store on each render, so it survives the re-listing that follows a create; it is announced as an `alertdialog` because dismissing it loses something irrecoverable; it is a read-only input rather than plain text so it can be selected and copied from the keyboard; and it is dismissed only when the operator says they have saved it, never by another request resolving. A browser that refuses clipboard access is not treated as an error — the secret is on screen and selectable, which is the fallback that matters.

Public clients are offered no secret rotation, since they have no secret. A disabled client says in as many words that its existing access tokens are refused too, because the audience is checked against the registry on every request and that is not obvious from the word "disabled". Redirect URIs are entered one per line and trimmed, with the exact-matching rule stated next to the field. The key section shows key ids and which one is signing — never key material — and says what rotating past the ring bound costs: tokens still in flight stop verifying.
